  "Number": 3,
  "Question": "If the length of a diagonal of a square is \\(a+b,\\) then the area of the square is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\((a+b)^2\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac12(a+b)^2\\)",
    "C": "\\(a^2+b^2\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac12(a^2+b^2)\\)",
    "E": "None of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Let the square's side length be \\(s>0\\). Its diagonal divides it into right triangles whose legs both have length \\(s\\). Since the diagonal has length \\(a+b>0\\), the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\\[\ns^2+s^2=(a+b)^2.\n\\]\nThe area of the square is \\(s^2\\), so there is no need to find \\(s\\) itself. Dividing by \\(2\\) gives the area\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac12(a+b)^2},\n\\]\nwhich is choice \\(\\boxed{B}\\).",
